## SECTION 3 — Redesigned Curriculum (8 Weeks + 2 Bonus Weeks)

> **Target:** Take absolute beginners to advanced ML engineering practitioners.  
> **Philosophy:** Every week = Reading + Hands-on Lab + Practice Exercises + End-to-End Project.

---

### 📅 WEEK 1 — Dev Environment & Version Control

**Goal:** Set up a professional, reproducible development environment; version-control everything.

**Topics:**
- VS Code: Extensions, settings sync, keybindings, devcontainers
- uv: Creating/managing Python projects, lockfiles, scripts vs packages
- Bash scripting: Variables, loops, pipes, cron jobs, `jq`, `awk`, `sed`
- Git & GitHub: Branching strategies, PRs, merge conflicts, `.gitignore`, `git bisect`
- SQLite: Schema design, queries, indexes, `sqlite-utils`, using SQLite as an app database
- GitHub Pages: Deploying static sites with custom domains and GitHub Actions

**Reading Material:**
- uv official docs — Projects section
- The Missing Semester of Your CS Education (Shell, Git chapters)
- SQLite Tutorial (sqlitetutorial.net)

**Hands-on Lab:**
- **Mini-Lab 1a:** Write a Bash script that pulls JSON from a public API, processes it with `jq`, and stores results in SQLite
- **Mini-Lab 1b:** Publish a personal portfolio site to GitHub Pages using a GitHub Action on push

**Practice Exercises:**
1. Migrate a requirements.txt project to uv with lockfile
2. Write a Bash script to auto-backup a SQLite DB to GitHub
3. Set up a GitHub Pages site with automated HTML generation from SQLite

**End-to-End Project:**
> Build a **Git-tracked personal data dashboard**: Bash script fetches public API data nightly (via cron/GitHub Action), stores in SQLite, generates an HTML report, and auto-deploys to GitHub Pages.

**New Topics to Add:**
- Bruno (API client replacing Postman — open source, git-friendly)
- `sqlite-utils` Python library for CLI-first database work
- GitHub CLI (`gh`) for PR and issue automation

---

### 📅 WEEK 2 — Containers, APIs & Deployment

**Goal:** Containerize and deploy applications to the web; understand API design.

**Topics:**
- FastAPI: Routes, path/query params, request bodies, middleware, background tasks, OpenAPI
- Docker: Dockerfile best practices, multi-stage builds, volumes, networking, `docker compose`
- Vercel: Deploying Python + Node serverless functions
- Render: Free-tier web service deployments, environment variables, persistent disks
- HuggingFace Spaces: Gradio + FastAPI spaces, Space secrets, GPU Spaces
- CORS, REST APIs, HTTP status codes
- Google Auth: OAuth2 flow, access tokens, service accounts

**Reading Material:**
- FastAPI official docs (Tutorial section)
- Docker Getting Started guide
- HuggingFace Spaces documentation

**Hands-on Lab:**
- **Lab 1: ChatBot with FastAPI** — Build a streaming chat API with FastAPI, Dockerize it, deploy to Render, add Google Auth for protected endpoints, embed a simple frontend on HuggingFace Spaces

**Practice Exercises:**
1. Add rate limiting and API key auth to a FastAPI app
2. Build a multi-container app (FastAPI + PostgreSQL) with docker compose
3. Deploy the same app to both Render and HuggingFace Spaces; document differences

**End-to-End Project:**
> **Public ChatBot Service**: Dockerized FastAPI backend with Google Auth login, rate limiting, chat history stored in SQLite, deployed on Render with the frontend on HuggingFace Spaces.

**New Topics to Add:**
- Pydantic v2 for input validation (deeply integrated with FastAPI)
- Health check endpoints and readiness probes
- Cloudflare Tunnel as free ngrok alternative

---

### 📅 WEEK 3 — Prompt Engineering & LLM Fundamentals

**Goal:** Master the art and science of communicating with LLMs; build reliable LLM pipelines.

**Topics:**
- Prompt engineering: Zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought, role prompting, delimiters
- Structured output: JSON mode, Pydantic + LLM, `instructor` library
- LLM text extraction: Named entities, tables from PDFs, receipts, invoices
- LLM sentiment & classification pipelines
- Function calling: Tool use, parallel tools, tool choice strategies
- `llm` CLI tool: Plugins, templates, conversation history, cost tracking
- Embeddings: What they are, cosine similarity, embedding models (text-embedding-3-small, Nomic)
- Base64 encoding for multimodal inputs

**Reading Material:**
- OpenAI Prompt Engineering Guide
- Anthropic's Prompt Engineering docs
- `instructor` library docs

**Hands-on Lab:**
- **Mini-Lab 3a:** Build a structured invoice extractor — upload PDF, extract line items as JSON using function calling + Pydantic
- **Mini-Lab 3b:** Build a CLI tool using `llm` with a custom prompt template and local model support via Ollama

**Practice Exercises:**
1. Compare zero-shot vs few-shot vs CoT on 10 tricky classification examples
2. Build an LLM evaluation harness: generate 20 test cases, run 3 models, compare outputs
3. Implement structured output with fallback retry logic using `instructor`

**End-to-End Project:**
> **Document Intelligence API**: FastAPI service that accepts any uploaded document (PDF/image), extracts structured data (entities, tables, summaries) using function calling, returns clean JSON.

**New Topics to Add:**
- Prompt caching (Anthropic/OpenAI) for cost reduction
- LLM cost tracking and token budgeting
- `outlines` library for guaranteed structured generation
- Streaming responses with Server-Sent Events

---

### 📅 WEEK 4 — RAG, Vector DBs & Hybrid Search

**Goal:** Build production-grade Retrieval Augmented Generation systems.

**Topics:**
- Chunking strategies: Fixed-size, recursive, semantic, document-aware, late chunking
- Vector databases: FAISS (in-memory), Chroma (persistent), PGVector (Postgres), Qdrant
- Hybrid search: Dense (vectors) + sparse (BM25/TF-IDF) combination
- Re-ranking: Cross-encoders, Cohere Rerank, ColBERT
- RAG evaluation: RAGAS framework — faithfulness, answer relevance, context precision/recall
- Multimodal embeddings: CLIP, image+text retrieval
- Topic modeling: BERTopic for corpus exploration

**Reading Material:**
- RAGAS documentation
- Chroma docs
- "Advanced RAG" blog series (Towards Data Science)

**Hands-on Lab:**
- **Lab 2: RAG ChatBot** — Build a full RAG pipeline: upload PDFs → chunk → embed into Chroma → retrieve with hybrid search (BM25 + vector) → re-rank → generate answer → evaluate with RAGAS
- **Lab 4: Hybrid RAG ChatBot** — Extend Lab 2 with PGVector (Postgres), add metadata filtering, multi-query retrieval, and a Streamlit/Gradio UI

**Practice Exercises:**
1. Compare 5 chunking strategies on the same document; measure retrieval quality
2. Benchmark FAISS vs Chroma vs PGVector on 10K document corpus
3. Implement RAGAS evaluation pipeline on your RAG system; iterate to improve scores

**End-to-End Project:**
> **Company Knowledge Base ChatBot**: Ingest a corpus of documents (PDFs, HTML pages, Notion exports), build a hybrid RAG system with PGVector, add conversation memory, evaluate with RAGAS, deploy as FastAPI + HuggingFace Space.

**New Topics to Add:**
- GraphRAG (Microsoft) — knowledge graph-enhanced retrieval
- Late chunking with JINA embeddings
- Contextual retrieval (Anthropic) — prepend chunk context before embedding
- Query rewriting and HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)

---

### 📅 WEEK 5 — Agentic AI & MCP

**Goal:** Build autonomous AI agents that use tools, call APIs, and run multi-step reasoning loops.

**Topics:**
- LLM Agents: ReAct loop, plan-and-execute, reflexion
- Pydantic AI: Defining agents, tool registration, structured agent output, dependency injection
- Function calling deep dive: Parallel tool calls, nested tools, tool error handling
- MCP (Model Context Protocol): Architecture, client/server model
- Setting up MCP server in VS Code (Claude Desktop + Claude Code)
- Building a custom MCP server in Python (FastMCP library)
- Multimodal agents: Vision + tool use (analyzing images, running code, web browsing)

**Reading Material:**
- Anthropic MCP specification (modelcontextprotocol.io)
- Pydantic AI documentation
- "Building LLM Agents" — LangGraph conceptual docs

**Hands-on Lab:**
- **Lab 6: AI Agent + MCP** — Build an autonomous research agent: given a question, it searches the web (Tavily/SerpAPI tool), reads URLs, stores findings in SQLite, and synthesizes a report — all via MCP server running in VS Code

**Practice Exercises:**
1. Build a file-system MCP server that an LLM can use to read/write local files
2. Implement a ReAct agent from scratch (no framework) using function calling
3. Build an agent that can debug its own errors by re-running and analyzing tracebacks

**End-to-End Project:**
> **Personal Research Assistant Agent**: An MCP server exposing tools (web search, URL reader, SQLite storage, PDF parser), deployed and accessible via Claude Desktop / VS Code. Saves research sessions with citations, exports to Markdown.

**New Topics to Add:**
- LangGraph for stateful multi-agent workflows
- Agent memory: short-term (context), long-term (vector store), episodic (logs)
- Agent evaluation: trajectory correctness, tool use efficiency
- OpenAI Swarm / CrewAI patterns for multi-agent coordination

---

### 📅 WEEK 6 — Media Processing & Computer Vision with LLMs

**Goal:** Process real-world media (images, video, audio) using modern vision-language models.

**Topics:**
